INT. ACCORDION REPAIR SHOP - NIGHT

Hanging bellows. Open instruments on workbenches, reeds spread like sleeping insects. A single lamp. The smell of glue and old felt.

OSWALDO PRENTISS-LAMB (50s, rolled sleeves, magnifying visor) bends over a half-disassembled accordion. In the doorway, rain on her coat, stands THEODORA "TEDDY" ASANTE (40s, a black case in her hand), who hasn't been here in years.

OSWALDO
We're closed.

TEDDY
You never close.

He takes off the visor. Sets it down. She sets the case on the counter and clicks it open: an accordion, scarred, one strap mended with fishing line.

OSWALDO
You kept it.

TEDDY
I kept everything.

He crosses to her slowly, wipes his hands on his apron, and lifts the instrument with both hands, turning it over like an infant. Teddy folds her arms, then unfolds them, then reaches out and touches the keys without pressing.

OSWALDO
The left side's wheezing.

TEDDY
It always wheezed.

OSWALDO
Not like this.

He presses the bellows. A long, tired breath. They both listen. A chord, soft and sour, hangs between them. Teddy closes her eyes. Oswaldo sets it down and hesitates, hands hovering.

TEDDY
Can you fix it?

OSWALDO
I could.

TEDDY
But?

OSWALDO
It's a better sound broken.

She laughs, a short, wet laugh, and wipes her eyes with her coat sleeve. He offers a rag. She takes it and does not use it. Rain drums the window. She picks up the case and shuts it, gently, over the instrument.

TEDDY
I'll come back for it.

OSWALDO
You said that before.

TEDDY
I know.

She walks out. The bell over the door rings. He stands at the counter with the open lid, listening to the last note fade from the felt.
